claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Sun, 11 Oct 2026 15:21:18 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> Review it and determine if it's ready to implement. If so, post a
>> patch. If not, request info necessary to get it ready.
>
> It is not ready yet. The workflow in the spec is clear, but three of
> its steps depend on things lab-claude does not have, and one depends
> on a convention we have not set. Each question below has a proposed
> answer. A plain "yes" to all of them is enough for me to post a patch.
>
> I read the spec (claude-skill-freebsd-port, e535c216), src/portia/
> spec.org, and oss/freebsd-ports/CLAUDE.md and Justfile. I also checked
> the lab-claude jail, and I searched the list, which has nothing on
> ports yet.
>
> 1. Ports tree ("base work off an up-to-date copy of ports tree")
>
>    The jail has no /usr/ports and no clone. The oss/freebsd-ports
>    clone recipe uses git@github.com:patmaddox/freebsd-ports.git over
>    ssh, and I have no ssh key. Proposal: the skill keeps its own jj
>    clone of https://github.com/freebsd/freebsd-ports.git at
>    ~/lab/freebsd-ports.jj, cloning it on first use. Each run does
>    "jj git fetch" and works on "jj new main@origin". Is that
>    location right, or would you rather mount a tree from the host
>    (it is about 1 GB)?

Updated in spec. I want the spec to be self-contained, not have some of
the information be in the spec and on the list.

> 2. Linters ("run all the linters")
>
>    portlint and portfmt (which provides portclippy) are not
>    installed in the jail. Proposal: I send a jails patch adding
>    devel/portlint and ports-mgmt/portfmt to lab-claude's packages,
>    as its own series ahead of the skill. The skill then runs the
>    same set as "just lint" in oss/freebsd-ports: portclippy,
>    "portfmt -D" (report a diff, never rewrite in place), and
>    "PORTSDIR=<tree> portlint -A". Is that the full set you want?

Installed

> 3. Distfiles and "do not build"
>
>    A port is not complete without distinfo, which "make makesum"
>    writes by fetching the distfiles. Proposal: fetching and makesum
>    are allowed, with DISTDIR under /tmp. Anything that extracts,
>    patches, or compiles is not. "make check-plist" needs a build,
>    so pkg-plist is drafted from the upstream install rules and
>    marked unverified in the patch notes for poudriere to confirm.

Updated spec

> 4. Posting ("post patch to list with [freebsd-ports] prefix")
>
>    format-patch.sh and process-email assume lab trunk as the base.
>    Proposal: the subject prefix is "[freebsd-ports PATCH]", so the
>    mail stays a patch to the notmuch rules and "just am". The patch
>    goes To: you, Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev, with base-commit naming the
>    ports tree commit. One port per patch, and related ports form a
>    series. Commit messages follow oss/freebsd-ports/CLAUDE.md:
>    "category/port: COMMENT" with pkg-descr and a WWW: trailer for
>    new ports, and "Update X => Y" with changelog links for updates.
>    There is no PR yet, so the PR: trailer is left out and you add it
>    when you open the bug. Is that right?

Updated spec

> 5. Relation to portia
>
>    src/portia already specifies new and update, plus lore.org with
>    porting conventions. Proposal: the skill replaces portia's new
>    and update paths. I fold the useful parts of lore.org into the
>    skill's reference files and leave portia untouched. Or should
>    the skill call portia instead?

Updated spec

> Two smaller points I will settle in the patch unless you object.
> "User invokable only" becomes "disable-model-invocation: true" in
> SKILL.md. "Latest good version" means the newest tagged release that
> upstream has not yanked or marked as a pre-release, checked against
> the release page or the registry and not only the tags.

Updated spec
